// Example sheet: everyday git.
# Git
$ git status            what changed
$ git add -p            stage changes piece by piece, answering y / n for each
$ git commit -m "MESSAGE"  commit what is staged
$ git commit --amend    fix the last commit (message or content) before pushing
$ git switch BRANCH     go to a branch (git switch -c NEW: create one)
$ git log --oneline --graph --all  history as a graph
$ git diff --staged     what is staged for the next commit
$ git stash             put changes aside (git stash pop: bring them back)
$ git restore FILE      throw away the changes to a file
$ git restore --staged FILE  unstage a file, keeping its changes
$ git push -u origin HEAD  push a new branch and track it
$ git reflog            every place HEAD has been: finds "lost" commits
